# Dictionary Library

On-device offline word lookup for PicoRead's Dictionary feature.

## Why not use StarDict's own .idx directly

StarDict (the format most free dictionaries, including FreeDict, ship in)
stores its index as variable-length records: `[word bytes][0x00][4-byte
offset][4-byte length]`, sorted case-insensitively. That variable length
rules out a random-access binary search on a RAM-constrained device: seeking
to an arbitrary byte offset can land inside the binary offset/length
fields (which legitimately contain 0x00 bytes) and misparse them as a word
terminator. The alternative - loading the whole index into RAM - doesn't
fit either (FreeDict's English-German index alone is ~10MB; the ESP32-C3
has ~380KB total).

This library instead reads a small custom fixed-width index
(`convert_freedict.py` produces it from a source StarDict dictionary), so
every record lives at `recordIndex * RECORD_SIZE` and binary search is a
plain seek - no resync heuristics, no full-index RAM load.

## On-device format

A dictionary is a folder (e.g. `/dictionaries/eng-deu/`) containing:

| File | Contents |
|------|----------|
| `dict.json` | `{"bookName": "...", "wordCount": N, "isHtml": true}` |
| `index.didx` | Sorted, fixed-width binary index (see below) |
| `entries.dict` | Raw definition text/HTML, copied through unchanged from the source StarDict `.dict` file |

### index.didx record layout (56 bytes each, `wordCount` records)

```text
[0..47]  word, UTF-8, NUL-padded (48-byte slot; words longer than this are
         dropped by the converter - see "Known limitations")
[48..51] uint32, little-endian: byte offset into entries.dict
[52..55] uint32, little-endian: byte length of the definition at that offset
```

Records are sorted case-insensitively (ASCII fold only - see limitations)
by word, ascending - this is inherited from the source StarDict index and
preserved as-is by the converter (dropping some records doesn't disturb
the order of the rest).

## Known limitations (accepted for v1)

- **ASCII-only case folding.** Lookups fold `A-Z` to `a-z` before
  comparing; accented letters (a German umlaut's uppercase/lowercase form,
  for instance) are compared byte-for-byte. This matches the overwhelming
  majority of real lookups (the reader only ever looks up one plain word
  at a time) and avoids pulling in a full Unicode case-folding table.
- **Words longer than 48 bytes are dropped**, not truncated - truncating
  would risk two different long entries colliding in the index. In
  practice these are virtually all full example-sentence "headwords" some
  Ding/FreeDict entries carry, never realistic targets for a single-word
  lookup UI (verified against the real eng-deu data: <0.001% of genuine
  single-word entries exceed 48 bytes).

## Producing a dictionary

```bash
python3 lib/Dictionary/scripts/convert_freedict.py <extracted-freedict-dir> <output-dir>
```

`<extracted-freedict-dir>` is a FreeDict "stardict" release, untarred (e.g.
`freedict-eng-deu-*.stardict.tar.xz`). Upload the resulting
`dict.json`/`index.didx`/`entries.dict` trio via the web menu's
Dictionaries page.
